Author: lora-dataset-studio <noreply@lora-dataset-studio.dev>
Date:   Mon Aug 3 10:20:33 2026 +0200

    feat(seedvr2): tile large upscales instead of running out of memory, and say the limit first
    
    Contributed by SurpassHR (GitHub #32), who hit a real CUDA out-of-memory
    upscaling a whole frame on an 11.6 GB card, and shipped a tiled workflow on his
    fork that reaches past 4K on the same hardware.
    
    TWO LANES, ONE OF THEM OPTIONAL.
    
    The default lane is unchanged in shape but no longer silent about its limit.
    Setup now shows roughly how many megapixels this GPU is good for in a single
    pass (VRAM x 0.55, deliberately conservative). It is guidance, never a gate: real
    headroom moves with the build, block swapping and whatever else holds VRAM, so a
    run that looks over budget still runs — it just says so first. On a card LDS
    cannot see it says nothing at all rather than inventing a number.
    
    The high-resolution lane cuts the frame into overlapping 1024 px tiles, upscales
    each and blends the seams back. It turns on by itself when the target exceeds the
    ceiling AND the tiling node pack is present; below the ceiling the frame stays
    whole, because tiling is not free. No new setting: the lane follows the geometry.
    
    WHAT WAS PORTED, AND WHAT WAS NOT.
    
    His graph chains three node packs. Two of them only do arithmetic — normalising
    a pixel count, dividing by 1024 to count tiles, resizing at the end — and one of
    those two, ComfyUI-Easy-Use, is GPL-3.0 while this repo is MIT and has refused a
    dependency over its licence before (ultralytics). None of that arithmetic needs
    to happen inside a graph, so it happens in tile_plan() instead, where it is also
    testable without a ComfyUI. The lane therefore depends on TTP alone: MIT, two
    classes, one probe. A test pins that reduction so a third pack cannot creep back.
    
    Node names were read from the pack's own NODE_CLASS_MAPPINGS rather than its
    README — in this very pack TTP_Tile_image_size maps to a class called
    Tile_imageSize, so the two spellings do not agree.
    
    Also taken from his graph, and useful on BOTH lanes: the DiT loader now offloads
    to system RAM between phases, and the VAE runs tiled encode/decode. Neither
    depends on the tiling pack.
    
    Backend: 20 new tests (tile geometry and its clamps, the ceiling and the reported
    11.6 GB case landing on the right side of it, lane choice with and without the
    pack, the graph's wiring, and a probe that fails CLOSED here — failing open would
    promise a lane whose nodes may not exist). Frontend: 8 new tests on the wording,
    including that an absent pack reads as optional and never as broken.
    
    Backend suite green, node --test 2127 passed. No real GPU run: the weights on the
    development machine are stubs, so this proves the SUBMITTED graph, not a render.
